{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 84, :comments ["<b>Twelve silver bowls</b> etc. Since he mentioned for each and every one one bowl, we would already know that their total was twelve; but the point is to say that whether one weighed them each individually or all together, the weight was equal, which is not so with ordinary vessels. And from this the Rabbis of blessed memory derived: not like the vessels of ordinary men are the vessels of the Eternal House — ordinary vessels, when one weighs them one by one and then goes back and weighs them all together, sometimes it comes out more, sometimes less." "<b>And behold,</b> each and every one of the chieftains intended in his offering that it should be like the offering of his fellow, and likewise in the measure of its weight. And the intention of each and every chieftain corresponded to his tribe. Nahshon reasoned that two royal houses were destined to come forth from him — Solomon and King Messiah — who are destined to rule over sea and dry land, and for this reason he brought a bowl, which is a model of the sea, for the sea surrounds the entire world and resembles a bowl. One hundred and thirty: on the third day the Holy One, blessed be He, gathered all the waters to one place and called them ימים (seas); ימי״ם in gematria is one hundred; He added one sea for the work of the Sanctuary, and a line of thirty would surround it — thus one hundred thirty. One mizrak, corresponding to the world, which is made like a sphere; and so you have in the bowl an allusion to the sea and in the mizrak an allusion to the inhabited world, which is the dry land. Seventy shekels, for these two kings are destined to rule over the seventy nations that are on sea and dry land. Both of them full — for they will bring offerings from all of them. Fine flour — those refined in gold. Mixed with oil — this is a good name, as is stated (Ecclesiastes 7:1): ״טוב שם משמן טוב״. Silver — as it says (Proverbs 10:20): ״כסף נבחר״ — the tongue of the righteous. One golden bowl of ten shekels — ten generations from Peretz to David, all of whom were righteous. Filled with incense — their deeds were as pleasing as the fragrance of incense. One bull — corresponding to Abraham; one ram — corresponding to Isaac; one lamb — corresponding to Jacob; one goat for a sin-offering — to atone for the deed of Judah, who brought the coat to his father; and for the sacrifice of well-being, two cattle — corresponding to David and Solomon, who established the kingship and were righteous and their kingship was whole; five rams, five he-goats, five yearling lambs — corresponding to the fifteen kings who reigned from Rehoboam until Zedekiah, who were each king son of a king; among them were completely righteous, among them intermediate, among them completely wicked. This was the reasoning of Nahshon. <b>Nethanel</b> son of Zuar reasoned a different reason in it, on the side of Torah, for the praiseworthiness of that tribe was in the wisdom of Torah: a silver bowl corresponding to the Torah, which is called bread, as is stated (Proverbs 9:5): ״לכו לחמו בלחמי״, and it is written regarding the bread of the Presence (Exodus 25:29): ״ועשית קערותיו וכפותיו״. <b>Zebulun</b> reasoned and offered on account of his commerce, for he would toil and place it into the mouth of Issachar and take his reward with him; a silver bowl corresponding to the sea — ״זבולן לחוף ימים ישכון״. And likewise each and every one, as is written in the Midrash: every tribe would aim and reason a different reason in its offering, and all of them agreed to one mind and to one offering, and in the measures of weight everything was for a known reason among them, each one expressing its own reason.<br><b>And what</b> need was there to write twelve passages, all of the same subject matter, for the offerings of the chieftains — and how many principles of Torah hang on a single word or a single letter? The correct reason for this is that the Holy One, blessed be He, grants honor to those who fear Him and honors those who honor Him, as He says (1 Samuel 2:30): ״כי מכבדי אכבד״. For had He mentioned and honored the first one and said, 'This is the offering of Nahshon son of Amminadab,' and then said, 'And thus did all the chieftains offer, each man on his day,' this would have been a curtailment of the honor of the others. Moreover, each one of them reasoned in his offering a reason of his own, unlike the reason of his fellow; and since the reasons were distinct — for each one of them alluded to his own tribe — therefore the passages came separately, for each and every one its own passage, in order to grant them honor. And to show that just as they were equal in their offering — in counsel, in the bringing of the offering, and in the measures of weight — so they were equal in merit. And since the honor of each one is equal in the eyes of the Place (HaMakom), therefore the Torah lengthens its account of each one of them equally. And even though it was revealed and known before the Holy One, blessed be He, that they were destined to corrupt themselves in the matter of Korah and to be among the two hundred and fifty, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not judge a person except according to his deeds at that hour, and He granted them honor because they offered for His honor." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the Torah needed to repeat the offerings of the twelve chieftains corresponding to the twelve constellations (mazzalot), and each and every constellation has its own sphere (galgal). Therefore each of the chieftains brought a bowl, which is round, for so too the sphere is round; and its weight was one hundred and thirty, corresponding to the count of the known stars; one mizrak of seventy shekels, for from the constellations are cast the occurrences, and the decrees come upon the seventy nations; one golden bowl of ten shekels, corresponding to the sphere of the Active Intellect (sekhel), which is exalted above all, and therefore it was of gold, because it is the most excellent of all the spheres — and further, on account of the holy forms there, namely the Ḥayyot and Seraphim, angels of fire, which resemble gold."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :register {:flags {0 ["the Eternal"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:57.396423707Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}